### 🟡 MISSING FEATURES / INCOMPLETE IMPLEMENTATIONS

**18. No Input Validation on Attack Endpoint**
`POST /api/attack/start` accepts `intensity` and `attackType` from request body without sanitizing them before passing to `node.startAttack(attackType, intensity)`. A large intensity value (e.g., 100,000 RPS) would spawn thousands of concurrent requests and could crash the load generator or the target nodes.

**19. No Authentication on Privileged Endpoints**
`/api/reset`, `/api/mode`, `/api/attack/start`, `/api/benchmark/run`, `/api/settings`, `/api/model/retrain`, and `/api/environment` — all require no credentials. Any client on the local network can trigger a reset, flip to manual mode, or launch load tests.

**20. Webhook Has No Rate Limiting or Debounce**
In high-traffic periods, every CRITICAL event calls `pushWebhookEvent()`. If dozens of events fire per second, the configured webhook server will receive a flood of HTTP POSTs. No debounce, no queue, no rate limit is applied.

**21. `learnFromIncident()` Doesn't Update Accuracy Metrics After Online Learning**
After the neural network is fine-tuned on a new incident sample, `this.accuracy`, `this.precision`, `this.recall`, and `this.f1Score` keep showing the pre-update held-out test values. The dashboard will display stale performance numbers after online learning.

**22. Orchestrator `activeIncidents` Map Grows Without Bounds**
Resolved incidents (RECOVERED, FAILED, CLOSED) remain in `this.activeIncidents` and `this.activeIncidentData` forever. Only a manual call to `orchestrator.reset()` clears them. Over long uptimes, this is a memory leak.

**23. `EventStore.calculateUptime()` — Permanently Returns 95–100%**
```js
const totalTime = 24 * 60; // Always 24 hours in minutes
return Math.max(95, Math.min(100, uptime)); // Clamped always between 95-100%
```
This metric is completely fabricated. It doesn't use `serverStartTime`, doesn't track actual downtime windows, and always returns a figure between 95% and 100% regardless of reality.

**24. `/api/receipts/latest` Returns Inconsistent Object Shapes**
```js
const latest = orchestrator.getStatus().latestIncident || getLatestReceipt();
```
When an in-flight incident is present, `latestIncident` is the raw `incidentBundle` object. When not, `getLatestReceipt()` returns a normalized receipt. The two objects have entirely different schemas — the frontend receives a receipt-shaped response sometimes and an incident-bundle other times.

